Package: faceid-nim
# NOT a literal architecture. This package ships a compiled daemon
# (faceid-nimd), a compiled PAM module, and a venv full of
# arch-specific binary wheels (onnxruntime, opencv, mediapipe) -- so it
# is architecture-dependent and dpkg must be told so, or it will happily
# install an x86-64 ELF on an arm64 machine where it cannot execute.
#
# "any" here describes the SOURCE, which really is architecture
# independent. override_dh_gencontrol in debian/rules replaces this
# line with $(DEB_HOST_ARCH) for the binary package, and restores the
# file afterwards. Do not "simplify" it to a literal arch or drop the
# override: with a literal value both CI matrix legs emit the SAME
# filename and one architecture silently replaces the other on the
# release page.
Architecture: any
Depends: ${shlibs:Depends}, ${misc:Depends}, libpam-runtime, python3 (>= 3.10),
 python3-gi, gir1.2-gtk-4.0, gir1.2-adw-1, gir1.2-gdkpixbuf-2.0,
 policykit-1 | polkitd, dbus, adduser, libglib2.0-bin
Recommends: gnome-shell (>= 45)
Suggests: linux-enable-ir-emitter
Description: Face unlock for Ubuntu with a lock-screen animation
 Face unlock via PAM, backed by a privileged daemon and an unprivileged
 vision worker, with a GNOME Shell pill showing scan state.
 .
 This is a convenience feature, not Face ID. On an ordinary RGB webcam
 it can be defeated by a good video replay; an infrared camera is
 substantially stronger. Your password always continues to work.
 .
 Face unlock is installed disabled and stays off until you enable it in
 the Face Unlock app.
